What this is
We're building a set of credentialing tasks for a fictitious healthcare organization called Coopa Blue Health. The purpose is training data: an AI agent is dropped into a realistic workplace, given a realistic mess, and has to reason its way to the professionally correct answer. Everything about the organization is invented, but everything about the rules it operates under — licensure, privileging, payer enrollment, federal identifiers — has to be real, because that's the reasoning being tested.

Coopa Blue is a nonprofit, two-hospital system in upstate New York: Coopa Blue Regional Medical Center (150 beds), Coopa Blue North (65 beds), roughly 1,800 employees and 249 credentialed providers. On 03/03/2025 it acquired Winston Imaging, a three-site outpatient imaging group with 24 providers, in an equity deal that left Winston's legal entity and billing identifiers alive. The unfinished integration is where every task comes from.

The agent plays Gordon Sawyer, a Senior Ops Analyst. He has OpenMRS, the clinical record system, which he can read and write, and Metabase, an analytics layer he can only read — plus a library of 50 documents numbered A01 through A50. The world's "now" is 04/13/2026, and nothing may be dated after it.

Each task has one profession-correct answer and a deliberate trap: a plausible wrong path a capable but careless agent would take.

What changed in this round
Two credentialing reviews came back. The first reorganized the whole world around four concepts that had been treated as interchangeable — registration currency, credentialing verification, medical-staff appointment and privileges, and payer enrollment and billing identifiers. A defect in one is never evidence about another, and several tasks now turn on telling them apart.

The second review caught three things the first missed, including one that made two of the first review's own recommendations contradict each other, and one that invalidated a task previously marked sound.

All ten tasks were rewritten. Two documents were renamed and re-attributed, one cast member was retitled, the canonical values were rebuilt, and every identifier in the world was regenerated.

Four problems that spanned the whole world
Every NPI in the spec was invalid
NPIs carry a check digit computed over the number prefixed with 80840. All nine identifiers in the original spec failed it, and across the full roster 232 of 254 failed — they had been invented as random digits. Any agent that validates an identifier, or any reviewer who spot-checks one, would have flagged the entire roster as synthetic.

The roster was regenerated rather than patched: every NPI now carries a valid check digit, and this is now an automated check so it cannot recur.

Touched every task and every data file

Who grants privileges — and the contradiction it exposed
The spec had the CMO granting privileges and the Compliance Committee issuing privileging rulings. Under CMS and New York rules that is the governing body's act, on the medical staff's recommendation. The chain is now consistent everywhere: Credentials Committee recommends → MEC forwards → governing body grants.

Following that through exposed a collision the first review didn't see. Extending T1 and T3 to include "suspend the provider" would have had the analyst performing a privileging act — the very thing the authority fix forbids. Resolved the second reviewer's way: the analyst applies administrative scheduling and billing holds and recommends precautionary suspension to the MEC, and never touches a privilege status.

T1, T3, T6, T7, T10 · documents A12, A13, A21, A25, A26

Individual and organizational NPIs were conflated
A Type 1 NPI identifies a person; a Type 2 identifies a billing organization. Nothing in the number distinguishes them. T4 had asserted the two were equivalent and instructed the agent to copy a personal identifier into a group field — which is precisely the defect T9 exists to catch. Both types are now labelled explicitly throughout, and T4's trap is now that very mistake.

T4, T5, T8, T9 · documents A08, A15, A16, A17, A19, A20

Missing data was being treated as proof
"No record in the log and none in OpenMRS" proves a repository gap, not that credentialing never happened. An evidence hierarchy now governs: governing-body record, then authoritative credentialing repository, then the legacy Winston archive, then the OpenMRS operational copy, then the tracking log. A negative in the bottom tiers is missing evidence, and only a documented negative at every applicable tier supports a true-gap finding.

Relatedly, discovering a defect no longer implies an automatic overpayment. The tasks now open a retrospective service and claims review and route anything identified through the applicable payer process, rather than asserting a universal six-year lookback across all payers.

