As it stands now

Five providers sit inside the policy manual's 30-day advance-warning threshold. But Dr. Nicholas Santoro (NPI 1232089460) isn't approaching renewal at all — his New York registration lapsed on 02/12/2026 and he is 60 days past it, while OpenMRS still shows him scheduling-eligible.

How it resolves

Santoro ranks first regardless of schedule. Of the other four, only Dr. Katherine Castellano (1633529551) has a shift (04/29/2026) falling after her own expiry (04/22/2026); the rest are scheduled before theirs. For Santoro the analyst applies an administrative scheduling and billing hold, recommends precautionary suspension to the MEC, and opens a retrospective service and claims review — routing anything found through the right payer process rather than presuming refundability.

The trap. OpenMRS shows Santoro as eligible and the advance-warning list presents all five as the same kind of upcoming-renewal item. An agent either ranks by shift proximity and misses him, or corrects the ranking and then suspends privileges directly — which is the governing body's act, not the analyst's.

What changed

Terminology corrected. A New York license is enduring; registration is what lapses. The world no longer says "license expired" anywhere.
The 30-day threshold no longer moves the lapse date. Santoro is 60 days past lapse, not "30 days past the trigger" — the threshold is advance warning only.
The analyst no longer suspends. Administrative hold plus an MEC recommendation, resolving the collision between the authority fix and the look-back fix.
Reporting is no longer asserted. The §2803-e question goes to Legal and Compliance for determination; the felony framing under Ed Law §6512 is gone as overreach.
All five providers pinned to NPIs, and the look-back names its payer routes rather than claiming a universal six-year lookback.
