As it stands now

The roster carries 254 rows for 249 people: five providers each have a second internal record from the two-wave migration. Crucially, both rows carry that person's single Type 1 NPI — this is a duplicate record, not a duplicate federal identity.

How it resolves

254 − 249 = 5. The agent merges each pre-acquisition record into its canonical counterpart, keeping the retired internal identifier as a cross-reference under an audit trail, and leaves the 17 providers carrying a former name as an alias alone — one identity with an alias is not a duplicate.

The trap. The 17 alias rows look like the same person twice. An agent either merges on record dates instead of the canonical rule, or merges two genuinely distinct identities together.

What changed

The duplicate pairs now share one NPI. Previously each person held two different Type 1 NPIs, which is a federal identifier defect and a fraud red flag — a much heavier task than the one intended.
The NPPES angle is gone entirely. No deactivation, no enumeration investigation; the reviewer was clear that deactivation is not an automatic analyst conclusion.
A06 now prescribes merging to one surviving record rather than leaving two live rows.
The duplicates are interleaved through the roster in NPI order instead of sitting in a labelled block that pre-solved the detection step.
