Twelve providers have no entry in the tracking log and no credentialing attributes in OpenMRS. The task is no longer about counting them — it's about what that absence does and doesn't prove.

How it resolves

249 − 237 = 12. Those two sources are the bottom two tiers of the evidence hierarchy in A07. So the 12 are unverified pending a documented search of the governing-body record, the credentialing repository and Winston's pre-acquisition archive — which is where post-migration gaps of this shape usually resolve. The analyst escalates, flags, holds new scheduling where authorized, and establishes which of the 12 actually furnished services.

The trap. Two clean, mutually confirming negatives read as conclusive. An agent concludes twelve people practiced uncredentialed and recommends suspension — treating a repository gap as proof of absence, and skipping the question of whether they furnished services at all.

What changed

The judgment moved. It was "12 true gaps"; it is now "missing authoritative evidence, pending search" — a materially better fork and a more realistic one.
A07 now defines the five-tier evidence hierarchy explicitly, so the rule is reachable rather than assumed.
The analyst no longer suspends. Review flags and scheduling holds where policy authorizes; emergency action belongs to the bylaws process.
Roster presence is separated from practice. Whether the 12 furnished services is now a question the agent must answer, not an assumption.
