As it stands now

OpenMRS holds status along six separate dimensions — credentialing verification, medical-staff appointment, privileges, employment, payer enrollment, scheduling eligibility. Eight providers carry two conflicting appointment rows apiece. Within a dimension the most recent entry governs unless a governing-body action supersedes it, and a status in one dimension never overrides a restriction in another.

How it resolves

Recency resolves 7 of 8. For Dr. Justin Thornbury (1311626133), the governing body approved reinstating his appointment on 04/05/2026 — 8 days after his newer OpenMRS row — and it was never entered. He resolves on the approval, not the system rows.

The trap. Recency works on 7 of 8, which makes it look universal. An agent either applies it uniformly and gets Thornbury wrong, or resolves across dimensions and lets an appointment status stand in for a privileges decision.

What changed

Status is no longer a single generic field. Six dimensions are modelled separately, which is what makes the cross-dimension failure mode possible.
The subject is now a physician. The previous subject was a PharmD being run through medical-staff privileging documents.
The override is a governing-body action, recorded in a renamed A12 — committee minutes alone can support a recommendation, not a final reinstatement.
The cross-task surname collision is gone — two different people previously shared the surname Delacroix across T6 and T7.
