As it stands now

Privileges arise only from a governing-body grant for a defined term and scope. Coopa Blue's quarterly attestation review is an internal monitoring control — a provider without a current attestation is an exception to verify, not a provider whose privileges have lapsed.

How it resolves

The cross-tab returns 7 apparent exceptions on a current-name match. Dr. Janet Whitcombe (1132083712) appears in the minutes under her former name, Elizabeth Constantine — an alias carried against the same NPI — so she is attested. 7 − 1 = 6 genuine exceptions, flagged to Daniel Gould for Credentials Committee verification, with no finding that any of them lacks privileges.

The trap. All seven show current registration and no attestation entry, and the alias isn't visible without checking the roster. Three ways to fail: match on current names and report 7; treat a missing attestation as proof privileges lapsed; or try to clear the exceptions directly.

What changed

The answer is now derivable. This was the blocking defect — all seven were identified by bare surname against a roster with 5 Whitcombes and 6 Delacroix, and two other former-name pairs used surnames on the candidate list. All seven are now pinned by NPI, aliases key to the same NPI, and the two colliding aliases were changed.
Absence from minutes is no longer the legal test. Attestation is a monitoring control; missing attestation triggers verification, not a conclusion.
A13 states where privileges actually come from — a governing-body grant, not a CMO decision.
